4.2. comparison with experimental results

Figures 8 and 9 present the mass flow and the pressure drop between the entrance and the exit of the

spiral with a heat load of 2.5 W on the spiral, the heater power being the varying parameter. The points

hold for the experimental results and the continuous line for the theoretical values. The mass flow (figure

8) is around 5 g/s if the tube heater is set to 1 W and it increases up to 7.5 g/s for the heater at 7 W with

a maximum difference of 6 % between the model and the experiment. When increasing the heater power,

the amount of vapor in the return pipe also increases and this increases the driving force and, then, the

mass flow. The pressure difference (figure 9) is between 1 mb and 1.5 mb for the heater between 1 W

and 7 W: as the heater power increases, the mass flow increases and then the pressure drop which is

quite well predicted with a maximum difference of 22 % between the model and the experiment for the

heater at 1 W. Globally, the simple model presents here reliable results to predict the mass flow and the

pressure difference of the thermosiphon operation as well as the influence of the tube heater.

